<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cretan Koinon</h1><p class="st-meta">216 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.) · capital Gortyn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Loose federation of the quarrelsome Cretan city-states, active in the Hellenistic period until the Roman conquest of the island in 69-67 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> was divided among dozens of independent cities, chief among them <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/knossos">Knossos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gortyn">Gortyn</a>, constantly at war. A common body of Cretans (koinon) appears in the 3rd century BCE, and around 216 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> was recognised as its protector (prostates), seeking influence over the island and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The koinon had a council and a common court (koinodikion) to settle disputes between cities, and issued joint decrees and treaties. It never overrode local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and wars such as the Lyttian War of the 220s BCE continued. Cretan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and Cretan ports harboured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The league was revived and dissolved several times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> war on piracy brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-antonius-creticus">Marcus Antonius</a>' failed attack in 71 BCE and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus</a>' conquest of the island in 69-67 BCE, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> became a Roman province with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gortyn">Gortyn</a> as its capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cretan-koinon.jpg" alt="Cretan Koinon" width="320" height="155"><figcaption>Image: Reinhard Saczewski · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koinon_der_Kreter_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett,_Berlin_-_5476853.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>216 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gortyn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Doric)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cretan League, Koinon of the Cretans</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-142&amp;focus=cretan-koinon" aria-label="Cretan Koinon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5312 910 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cretan Koinon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5632 1166L5634 1166M5632 1166L5637 1158M5632 1166L5607 1150M5632 1166L5652 1167M5632 1166L5625 1157M5632 1166L5598 1152M5632 1166L5610 1152M5632 1166L5650 1162M5632 1166L5552 978M5632 1166L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5634 1166h0M5637 1158h0M5607 1150h0M5652 1167h0M5625 1157h0M5598 1152h0M5610 1152h0M5650 1162h0M5552 978h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5632 1166h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5632 1166h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gortyn. <a href="/atlas?year=-142&amp;focus=cretan-koinon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

